  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A drying and cooling to seasonal temperatures\nafter today.\n\nA backdoor front has well passed the region today. Best radar\ncoverage of showers/storms runs from northeast TN into southern\nNC in association with some upper divergence and a vort max\ntracking through the southern Appalachians. Another upstream\nshortwave and inverted trough behind the front should focus\nscattered to numerous coverage of showers/few storms from the NC\nmountains into southern WV this afternoon. Best instability for\nsevere parameters overall should stay south of our forecast \narea, but some stronger storms with frequent lightning could \noccur especially along/south of the VA border. \n\nThe inverted trough lingers into Monday although shower trends\nwill drop mainly confined to far southwest VA into NW NC into\nMonday after this evening.\n\nShould see more sunshine and lower humidity Monday into Tuesday.\n\n\n\nKey Message 2: Heat builds again mid to late week but not as hot as last\nweek. A chance of storms Thu-Sat.\n\n594dm heights return by midweek with more of a westerly flow.\nShould see temps warm about 5 to 10 degrees above normal, still\n\"hot\" for September but not looking as hot as last week.\n\nA front tracks southeast into the area Thursday with a decent\nshot of shower/storms. Depending on timing of this feature would\nnot be surprised to some stronger storms Thu afternoon/evening.\n\nFront track south into the Carolinas but stalls as it becomes\nparallel to upper flow. Another wave of low pressure across the\nsoutheast could keep shower chances around into the weekend.",
